# 05 · Limitations and assumptions

**This is a modeled projection, not silicon.** We designed MDA-1 only to the block level. We did not
synthesize it (except for the router sketch), and we did not place, time or fabricate it. Each MDA-1
number and each GPU-software number is the output of a Python performance model. The list below
gives the inputs of this model.

We quote the GPU numbers with the label *measured* from the
deepseek-ultrafast README. We did not run the engine or profile GPUs.

## What is validated, and how far

| claim | validation | strength |
|---|---|---|
| model shapes and bytes | parameter counts, activated params and 890 B/token KV from checkpoint headers ([01](01_workload.md#2-architecture-numbers)) | strong |
| GPU decode step model | 2-parameter fit to 2 points. Leave-one-out {{ "%+.1f"|format(D.calibration.loo.b300_tp8.error_pct) }}% / {{ "%+.1f"|format(D.calibration.loo.gb300_tp4.error_pct) }}%, but low power: any η prior from 0.70 to 0.90 passes with an error of ~3% or less | **weak** |
| "about half the step is fixed latency" | {{ K.fixed_lo|pct }}-{{ K.fixed_hi|pct }} across 10 refits, which include the README's run-to-run extremes; consistent with published barrier and all-reduce latencies | moderate. Not confirmed by a profile; `tools/model_vs_nsys.py` prints the prediction in the engine's own `nsys_breakdown.py` groups. The fit does **not** identify the split between collectives and other stages |
| GPU dataflow-style software ≈ MDA-1 speed | same simulator, same-physics constants; Monte Carlo {{ K.MC.vs_sw.p5|x(2) }}-{{ K.MC.vs_sw.p95|x(2) }} | moderate. Nobody built the software or the chip |
| MDA-1 decode speed | bottom-up; joint Monte Carlo {{ K.MC.vs_gpu_measured.p5|x(2) }}-{{ K.MC.vs_gpu_measured.p95|x(2) }} vs measured | **unvalidated**: no silicon, no RTL beyond the router |
| matrix-engine utilization | SCALE-Sim cross-check (led to a 1.6x correction) | moderate (compute stage only) |
| router latency | RTL verified in simulation, {{ RTL.match }} vs a golden model | strong for function, weak for timing (not synthesized) |
| prefill / TTFT | **not validated**: measured prefill is {{ P.prefill.fit_k_tp4|n(1) }}x slower than our model; TP scaling mispredicted by {{ "%+.0f"|format(P.prefill.pred_tp8_err_pct) }}% | weak; reported for completeness |
| cost, energy | first-order, sourced constants where they exist; GPU power and price are assumptions, thus we report ratios as ranges | weak to moderate |

## Every assumption

[sources.md](sources.md) gives a key for each sourced constant. **A** marks an assumption that has
no public source. We sweep the important assumptions (tornado, Monte Carlo, or the GPU price and
power tables in [04](04_results.md#cost)).

**Workload**
- Batch 1, context about 2.6K, acceptance 5.6 tokens/step (README mean). The README's 1,740 tok/s x 3.30 ms implies 5.74. The README's range goes up to 6.1. This is more than the 6-row verify maximum, thus its definition is probably different. We sweep 5.1-6.0. We model verify at 6 rows, but the engine adapts the length.
- **A** Expert routing is independent across the 6 rows of a step (`locality = 0`). A locality of 0.5 decreases the bytes on every platform. The calibration then needs η = 0.53.
- **A** The kernel stages for each op (`Op.n_kernels`, {{ W.kernels_per_step }} per step) are our interpretation of `model.rs`, not a trace. We treat all-reduces as fused into producers (no extra stage). Side-stream work (indexer weights, compressor, mHC dots, `main_proj`) has no stage on the critical path. Only the product with κ is calibrated.
- Expert bytes on the GPU baseline agree with the bytes that the engine's tensor maps read (`model.rs::sgl_init`). Routed experts stream only the 64-row padding of w2 (+{{ ((W.layout_factor.tp8-1)*100)|n(1) }}% at TP8, 0 at TP4). The FP8 copy of the shared expert streams its 128-row padding.
- The decode indexer scan and top-k run as replicas on each rank, as in the engine (`model.rs::idx_rows`).
- Engram tables are in GPU HBM on the baseline, as in the engine, and in LPDDR on MDA-1. We do not model the vision tower.

**GPU baseline and projections**
- Peak specs come from NVIDIA/OEM pages (GB300 8 TB/s; HGX B300 7.7 TB/s, 4.5 PF FP8 dense). Calibrated: η = {{ D.calibration.hbm_eff|n(3) }}, κ = {{ D.calibration.kernel_overhead_us|n(2) }} µs per dependent stage.
- The prior for the all-reduce latency is 2.37 µs (sourced). The engine's one-shot NVLS design is nearest to this value. The alternatives of 5 and 10 µs fit equally well.
- **A** GPU power is 60% of TDP during batch-1 decode (swept 40-100%). We did not measure it. Thus each tokens/J ratio that includes a GPU is a range.
- **A** The owned cost of a GPU is $45K all-in (no public B300/GB300 price; swept $25-65K). The rental price, $9.50/GPU-hr, is sourced.
- **A** The dataflow-style software projection uses these constants:
  - a 0.5 µs flag hand-off (sourced L2 round trip 0.306 µs plus tail);
  - a 0.5 µs router;
  - a 64 MB L2 staging slice (L2 size not sourced);
  - the same DRAM first-byte latency as MDA-1 (0.5 µs);
  - the same 2.37 µs all-reduce as today's baseline.
- Nobody demonstrated the dataflow-style software projection for this model. Its largest step assumes a stage hand-off of ~1 µs instead of the fitted {{ D.calibration.kernel_overhead_us|n(1) }} µs. The fit cannot confirm that this value is possible.
- Rubin: 19.2 TB/s (NVL72 page; the HGX page says 22). **A** 1.8 kW power. We use today's software constants with no change.

**MDA-1**
- Package: 2 dies x 4 HBM3E 8-Hi at 8 TB/s total (sourced stack specs, run below their rating). η = the GPU's calibrated value (same HBM).
- **A** We sweep all of these constants jointly:
  - HBM cube shoreline 11 mm;
  - per-op hand-off 0.3 µs, with die crossing;
  - dependent-fetch latency 0.5 µs;
  - router 0.1 µs (RTL 65 ns plus margin);
  - collective 2.57 µs (the best measured 4-GPU all-reduce, 2.37 µs, plus 0.2 µs die crossing; no link advantage assumed).
- **A** Compute: 2 PF FP8 / 4 PF FP4 at 1.5 GHz, matrix efficiency 0.5 (SCALE-Sim-corrected), 16-row engine.
- **A** Area:
  - SRAM 0.5 mm²/MiB (sourced density x2);
  - FP8 MAC 80 µm², SerDes 0.7 mm²/lane (72 x 112G) and die-to-die 10 mm²/die have no source;
  - HBM PHY 12 mm² comes from an unverified GUC figure.
- Energy:
  - HBM 4.0 pJ/bit (HBM2 figure);
  - SRAM 0.5 pJ/bit (**A**);
  - NoC 0.5 pJ/bit (**A**);
  - 112G link 5 pJ/bit (sourced);
  - die-to-die 0.25 pJ/bit (sourced);
  - MAC energies from 7 nm tables x2 (**A**);
  - static 150 W per package (**A**);
  - 88% power-delivery efficiency (**A**).
- Cost: wafer $17K, D0 0.10, HBM3E $15/GB, CoWoS-L ~$2.1K plus test (all sourced). NRE $300M (**A**, in the sourced $280-542M range), volume 20K (**A**), system share $8K per package (**A**). This is the manufacturing cost, with no margin and no respin. The comparison is fair for a build-vs-buy decision, not for a merchant chip.
- Not simulated:
  - the NoC at flit level;
  - HBM at bank/row level (efficiency factor only);
  - thermal and power delivery beyond a loss factor;
  - refresh, ECC/RAS;
  - the fabric that is necessary for more than 4 packages.

**Not modeled at all**:
- request scheduling and queueing;
- prefix-cache hit rates;
- KV eviction and the SSD tier;
- vision;
- the effects of reasoning effort on output length;
- failures.

The static-schedule compiler is out of scope. Both MDA-1 and the dataflow-style software need it. It is the largest
engineering risk.

## What can change the conclusions

- **A profile that shows that the GPU step is mostly HBM-bound** (fixed share much less than {{ K.fixed_lo|pct }}). The 2x software and silicon headroom then decreases accordingly.
- **A demonstrated dataflow-style runtime** confirms the software path and makes the silicon case weaker than it is now. A runtime that stays much slower than the projection makes the silicon case stronger.
- **Measured GPU power** replaces the least certain assumption behind the tokens/J ratio.
- **GPU prices less than about $30K**, a volume less than about {{ K.be_sw_300|n(-3) }} packages ($300M NRE), or an NRE near $600M. In each of these cases, MDA-1 does not cost less per token than GPUs that run the same software.

<a id="revisions-after-adversarial-review"></a>

## Revisions after adversarial review

An independent reviewer reviewed the study twice. The reviewer had the instruction to find the
points that experts can object to. We addressed each point as follows.

**Round 1**

1. The lead of MDA-1 over "ideal GPU software" came from asymmetric constants. The platforms now share the same-physics constants. The software projection is a projection, not a bound. The result is parity (Monte Carlo above).
2. It is not possible to build eight HBM3E stacks on one 448 mm² die. MDA-1 is now a 2-die package with 11 mm of shoreline for each stack.
3. The HBM efficiency of MDA-1 was *above* the GPU's calibrated value, and the text called it conservative. It now uses the same value.
4. The study reported robustness one parameter at a time. It now reports a joint Monte Carlo with the worst corner.
5. The study presented the fixed-latency share as tighter than the data allow. It is now a range over 10 refits, which include the run-to-run extremes. The study no longer dismisses the low-efficiency alternatives (B200 STREAM is ~52% at 4-16 GB arrays).
6. The GPU baseline ignored the engine's fusions (PDL, producer-pushed NVLS all-reduce, side streams). It also ignored the padded expert layout (+26% bytes at TP8) and the replicated decode indexer. The model now includes all of them. The term "kernel launch" became "dependent-stage latency".
7. The cost comparison mixed rental and owned bases in one column. It had no prefill-pool cost, and it used 2026 HBM4 prices for a 2027 part. We corrected these errors. Energy now includes NoC and power-delivery loss. The ratios are ranges over GPU power.
8. The Rubin and 8-chip comparisons gave today's overheads to the GPUs and gave MDA-1 its own overheads. Both comparisons now include a same-software GPU row. We removed the 8-chip MDA-1 headline.
9. Text errors:
   - B300 is 7.7 TB/s (MDA-1 matches GB300's 8);
   - the batch-256 comparison was inverted;
   - the 1.4 µs all-reduce is a computed bound for 2 x GB200, not a measurement;
   - per-GPU byte tables were inconsistent between pages;
   - acceptance cannot be more than 6 with a 5-token draft.

**Round 2**

10. The padding correction from round 1 was wrong. The tensor maps of the routed experts never read the padded w13 rows. The model now streams only the row padding of w2 and the padding of the shared expert. This changed η from 0.774 to {{ D.calibration.hbm_eff|n(3) }} and changed the central fixed share to {{ K.fixed4_share|pct }}.
11. The silicon-vs-software Monte Carlo drew the GPU's hand-off and router from ranges with centres above their base values. The centre of each range is now the base value of its platform.
12. The software projection used a 2-GPU *computed* all-reduce bound on 4 GPUs. Each platform now uses the measured 2.37 µs. MDA-1 now adds the die-to-die crossing costs that it omitted before.
13. The ablation gave the credit to "removing barriers" for a step that adds barriers. The text now calls it the persistent-program step and flags it as the least certain step. We removed the separate "speed-of-light all-reduce" step.
14. The text attributed the gain of Rubin to bandwidth, but it compared different software. The study now reports same-software ratios. The $/token ratios include the prefill adder. We removed the "+X% at 16 chips" claim on a fabric that is not designed. Text corrections: "ceiling" → "projection", "for free" → "without new silicon", and the rendered text "-0%".
